WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.700
Fans in stormtrooper costumes, a hugely secretive&nbsp;
production, and major shade from the director of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.845
"Gremlins." The truth about "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;
might shock even the most dedicated fans.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.940
When the first episode of "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;
hit Disney+ in 2019, it came with a very big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.140
reveal. As well as debuting a season-long&nbsp;
plotline that hadn't been mentioned in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.160
media or trailers that preceded the series,&nbsp;
"The Mandalorian" worked hard to ensure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.120
the existence of Baby Yoda would be kept secret&nbsp;
before the show's premiere. The first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.740
of course, focuses on Din Djarin, a solitary&nbsp;
Mandalorian who must transport and protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.580
"The Child," a cute creature that was&nbsp;
quickly nicknamed "Baby Yoda" by fans.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.480
Series creator Jon Favreau decided to&nbsp;
remain secretive about the project after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.200
heeding the words of Donald Glover,&nbsp;
who played Lando Calrissian in "Solo:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.840
A Star Wars Story." Favreau&nbsp;
explained to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.960
"[Glover] was saying that what people&nbsp;
really like now is to be surprised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.540
because it doesn't happen that much&nbsp;
[...] When Beyoncé did an album,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.800
she would just put it online and everybody would&nbsp;
react to it. Just putting it out there spurred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.700
a conversation that would become more viral and&nbsp;
bring more genuine attention than any marketing."

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.820
Disney executives agreed with the&nbsp;
approach, and kept all imagery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.164
Baby Yoda, who was eventually named&nbsp;
Grogu, out of marketing materials.

00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:11.760
Before "The Mandalorian," Pedro Pascal was best&nbsp;
known for his role on "Game of Thrones." When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.180
producers first approached him about playing&nbsp;
the lead role in "The Mandalorian," he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.800
apparently reluctant. In fact, Pascal's close&nbsp;
friend Oscar Isaac, who plays Poe Dameron in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.800
the "Star Wars" sequel trilogy, was the&nbsp;
one who convinced him to take the role.

00:01:22.905 --> 00:01:24.885
"You're welcome. I convinced him to do it."

00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:29.520
The Jedi Ahsoka Tano, who previously appeared&nbsp;
in multiple animated "Star Wars" projects, first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.600
appears in Season 2 of "The Mandalorian." For the&nbsp;
live-action version of the character, producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.160
hired Rosario Dawson following a successful&nbsp;
viewer campaign. She later told Vanity Fair,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.792
"It first came from fans online.&nbsp;
Someone tweeted me and fan-cast&nbsp;me.

00:01:41.792 --> 00:01:44.885
 I retweeted back and I was&nbsp;
like, 'Absolutely, yes please.'"

00:01:44.885 --> 00:01:50.345
"Is he speaking? Do you understand him?"

00:01:50.345 --> 00:01:52.740
Someone on the "Star Wars" publicity team saw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.340
exchange and sent the thread to producer&nbsp;
Dave Filoni, and the rest is history.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.060
Some prestigious actors rounded out the&nbsp;
cast, with Academy Award nominee Nick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.620
Nolte appearing in an unforgettable role. Carl&nbsp;
Weathers, noted for his performance as Apollo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.720
Creed in "Rocky," portrays Greef Karga, while&nbsp;
former MMA star and American Gladiator Gina&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.280
Carano played Cara Dune in the show's first&nbsp;
two seasons. Critically acclaimed director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.840
Werner Herzog stepped in as a pro-Empire crime&nbsp;
lord. Meanwhile, "Breaking Bad" and "Better Call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.700
Saul" alum Giancarlo Esposito joined the&nbsp;
cast as ex-Imperial officer Moff Gideon.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.680
The creative team behind "The Mandalorian" took&nbsp;
inspiration from many mid-20th-century works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.840
but they may have borrowed a&nbsp;
little too liberally from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.100
more recent pop culture entries. Joe Dante&nbsp;
directed both "Gremlins" and "Gremlins 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.060
The New Batch," and he believes that Baby&nbsp;
Yoda owes a lot to Gizmo, the furry little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.720
creature from his films. Speaking to&nbsp;
the San Francisco Chronicle, Dante said,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.780
"I think the longevity of ['Gremlins']&nbsp;
is really key to this one character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.200
who is essentially like a baby [...] Which&nbsp;
brings me, of course, to the subject of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.820
Baby Yoda, who is completely stolen and is just&nbsp;
out-and-out copied. Shamelessly, I would think."

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.720
And then there's "American Dad!" which has&nbsp;
been running since 2005. In the episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.720
"Persona Assistant," which first aired in February&nbsp;
2019, the alien character Roger develops a tumor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.680
which turns out to be a strange, sentient,&nbsp;
miniature version of himself. He names the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.920
monster, which becomes a recurring&nbsp;
"American Dad!" character, Rogu.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.060
"His name's Rogu."

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.507
"Ro-guuuuu."

00:03:14.507 --> 00:03:16.560
While Baby Yoda appears in the first episode of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.700
"The Mandalorian," the name Grogu wasn't&nbsp;
revealed until November 2020. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.230
it's undeniable that both alien&nbsp;
babies have nearly identical names.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.310
"Grogu?"

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.200
Season 1 of "The Mandalorian" wraps up with a&nbsp;
confrontation between the titular bounty hunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.100
and villain Moff Gideon. The Mandalorian&nbsp;
refused to return Grogu to his dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.160
intended recipient, and had to&nbsp;
contend with a veritable army of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.340
stormtroopers. A lot of anonymous foot&nbsp;
soldiers were required for the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.000
so many that the production couldn't come up&nbsp;
with enough authentic stormtrooper costumes.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.320
According to ComicBook.com, the makers of "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" took their problem to "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.720
fans. Producers Dave Filoni and Jon Favreau called&nbsp;
upon the Chicago chapter of the 501st Legion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.460
a devoted "Star Wars" fan and cosplay club. Its&nbsp;
members appeared in the final two episodes of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:04.020
Mandalorian" Season 1, dressed in their privately&nbsp;
owned, film-quality stormtrooper costumes which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.188
they'd purchased, built, and maintained,&nbsp;
which is more than a little impressive.

00:04:08.188 --> 00:04:12.060
"The Mandalorian" is the first live-action&nbsp;
"Star Wars" series and it was the first original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.100
series to debut on Disney+ when the streamer&nbsp;
launched in 2019. According to Vanity Fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.180
when "Star Wars" production company Lucasfilm&nbsp;
decided to pursue small-screen storytelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.280
president Kathleen Kennedy commissioned input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.400
from "Iron Man" director and known&nbsp;
"Star Wars" enthusiast Jon Favreau.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.920
Favreau got to work after a&nbsp;
single meeting with Kennedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:32.460
writing the first four episodes of a show&nbsp;
about a Mandalorian, a member of a race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.640
of helmet-wearing bounty hunters. There&nbsp;
could have been an issue with overlap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.020
as Lucasfilm was also developing a movie about the&nbsp;
best-known pre-existing Mandalorian in the "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.100
Wars" saga, Boba Fett. However, the problem&nbsp;
disappeared when director, James Mangold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.540
walked away from the Boba Fett movie to make the&nbsp;
fifth "Indiana Jones" film. However, Dave Filoni,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.320
the creative force behind several animated&nbsp;
"Star Wars" projects, also wanted to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.940
TV show about a Mandalorian. Kennedy put Favreau&nbsp;
and Filoni in a room, and they got along so well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.380
creatively that they combined their ideas into a&nbsp;
single project, which became "The Mandalorian."

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.280
As it's set during the storyline&nbsp;
of the original "Star Wars" saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.060
"The Mandalorian" is obviously inspired&nbsp;
by classic science fiction. Creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.840
writers, and producers also pulled heavily&nbsp;
from other sources, including Westerns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:18.060
20th century world cinema, and Japanese manga.&nbsp;
Star Pedro Pascal told Entertainment Weekly,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.380
"The Mandalorian is a mysterious, lone gunfighter&nbsp;
in the outer reaches of the galaxy. Some might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.920
say he has questionable moral character,&nbsp;
in line with some of our best Westerns."

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.120
The main character's identity is shrouded in&nbsp;
secrecy, which is not unlike the Man with No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.260
Name in Sergio Leone's "Dollars Trilogy"&nbsp;
starring Clint Eastwood. Series producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.120
and writer Dave Filoni also admitted to paying&nbsp;
homage to Japanese filmmaker Akira Kurosawa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.860
who influenced "Star Wars" creator George Lucas.&nbsp;
In an interview with /Film, Filoni explained,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:48.360
"George's mentor is Akira Kurosawa. And so I've&nbsp;
done a lot of study of that [...] I have a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.620
small Akira Kurosawa figure and he is in every&nbsp;
episode I've directed somewhere in the set."

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.480
"The Mandalorian" also appears to crib from "Lone&nbsp;
Wolf and Cub," a long-running manga series adapted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:02.160
into three films. Like Mando and Grogu's toiling&nbsp;
around the galaxy, "Lone Wolf and Cub" concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.720
a ronin who roams the wilderness with a child he&nbsp;
protects when they run afoul of powerful forces.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.620
The entire "Star Wars" universe&nbsp;
owes a huge debt to George Lucas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.860
but "The Mandalorian" has some specific&nbsp;
ties to the filmmaker. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.340
the show is heavily inspired by the first&nbsp;
"Star Wars" movie, which Lucas directed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.780
especially the episode's cantina scene. "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" also uses technology originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.100
developed for the "Star Wars" prequels, with&nbsp;
20-plus years of technical advancements added in.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.940
Giancarlo Esposito, who portrays Moff&nbsp;
Gideon on "The Mandalorian," revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.240
that Lucas' input on the series goes&nbsp;
even deeper. Speaking to Collider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.780
Esposito discussed the creation of&nbsp;
the show's costumes and sets, saying,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.440
"[Jon Favreau was] working with George&nbsp;
Lucas on this particular piece and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.260
it a piece that we can really relate&nbsp;
to now in our world that we’re in."

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.160
While Jon Favreau and his team used&nbsp;
practical effects as much as they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.460
to capture the hand-made charm of the&nbsp;
original "Star Wars," digital wizardry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.300
was key to bringing the exotic world of "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" to life. It's worth noting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.900
Favreau has a fair amount of experience&nbsp;
working on primarily digital movie sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.800
as he's the person behind Disney's live-action&nbsp;
"The Lion King" and "The Jungle Book" remakes.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.700
To create the jaw-dropping special effects seen&nbsp;
in "The Mandalorian," the filmmakers utilized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:10.080
the impressive Volume technology, which&nbsp;
can turn any soundstage into a 360-degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.860
panorama. With the ability to render&nbsp;
detailed 3D environments in real time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.880
the team was able to create digital sets&nbsp;
that felt truly immersive. Discussing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.480
approach to using new technologies to&nbsp;
create the show, Favreau told Variety,

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.140
"Ultimately, it’s the human factor that&nbsp;
shapes our path into the future and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.020
the people who are innovating in&nbsp;
those areas, creating new tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.900
that are going to determine what we&nbsp;
inherit and what the future holds."

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.780
At Star Wars Celebration 2019, Jon Favreau&nbsp;
repeatedly emphasized that "The Mandalorian" would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.220
be one giant love letter to the original "Star&nbsp;
Wars" trilogy, and that tribute extends behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:45.000
scenes as well. Favreau wants to make sure "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" feels like our first visit to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.260
galaxy far, far away, and that means embracing&nbsp;
some old-school film production techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:54.000
Naturally, CGI and special effects are necessary&nbsp;
to deliver "The Mandalorian" on a TV budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.440
but the crew is also using puppets, animatronics,&nbsp;
and special effects makeup whenever possible.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.560
For example, Favreau really wanted&nbsp;
to make a model of the Razor Crest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.340
the Mandalorian's ship, to use during&nbsp;
production. Industrial Light &amp; Magic's art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.780
staff were apparently so excited about the idea&nbsp;
that they didn't just make a model. Unprompted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.800
they reportedly went home and built a camera&nbsp;
rig in their garages, recreating the hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.820
that was used to make "Star Wars" back&nbsp;
in 1977. With a team that dedicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:21.960
it's unsurprising that "The Mandalorian" became&nbsp;
an instant hit with viewers all over the world.

